const llmActionsProps = {"enabled":true,"providers":["claude","chatgpt","perplexity","gemini"],"placement":"header","copyButton":true,"footerLink":true,"markdownBody":"# Connecting an AI assistant {#connecting-an-ai-assistant}\n\nThe editor can provide its operations to an AI assistant over the Model Context Protocol. The assistant then works on your project through the same commands the editor and the command line use, rather than by reading and rewriting files as text.\n\nThis setup differs from using an agent inside the editor. The assistant runs in a terminal or desktop application, and the editor provides the tools.\n\nYou can connect through the running editor or start an MCP server for a project folder without opening the editor. Both modes provide document and project operations. Tools that control the editor require a running editor.\n\n## Turn on the server {#turn-on-the-server}\n\nThe integration server is off by default.\n\n:::steps\n1. **Open the settings** Select **File > Settings**.\n2. **Enable the server** Select **Server**, and then enable the server.\n3. **Confirm it is listening**\n\n   ```bash\n   dogsbay-xml status\n   ```\n:::\n\nThe server binds to localhost and requires a token, which is written to a discovery file in your home directory so local clients can find it.\n\n## Connect a client {#connect-a-client}\n\nThe editor prints the configuration for you:\n\n```bash\ndogsbay-xml status --mcp-config\n```\n\nFor Claude Code, it can print the command that registers it:\n\n```bash\ndogsbay-xml status --mcp-install\n```\n\nPaste the configuration into the client's MCP settings, or run the command.\n\n## Without the editor {#without-the-editor}\n\nThe same tools run with no editor open, on the files of a project folder:\n\n```bash\nclaude mcp add dogsbay -- dogsbay-xml mcp --root /path/to/project\n```\n\nAlternatively, add the following configuration to `.mcp.json`:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"mcpServers\": {\n    \"dogsbay\": {\n      \"command\": \"dogsbay-xml\",\n      \"args\": [\"mcp\", \"--root\", \"/path/to/project\"]\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWhen the server runs without the editor:\n\n- The agent can read and write only within the specified project folder. It cannot switch projects.\n- The agent must read a file before editing it. The server rejects the edit if the file has changed since that read.\n- Edits are recorded as proposals attributed to the client. Accept or reject them later in the editor or with `dogsbay-xml review`. To apply edits without proposal markup, pass `--no-proposals` and review the changes in version control.\n- Tools for the cursor, selection, open documents, Author view, and screenshots are unavailable.\n- The server does not write to files that have unsaved changes in a running editor.\n\nThe server writes the audit log to the project's `.dogsbay/agent-audit/` directory.\n\nTo run a single tool from a terminal or script, use `dogsbay-xml tool <name> '<json>'`. The command prints the tool's JSON result.\n\n## What the assistant can do {#what-the-assistant-can-do}\n\nThe tools support the same operations as the command line:\n\n- **Read and validate** documents, including Schematron and project-wide validation\n- **Inspect the project** to find where a file is used, what a key resolves to, which references are broken, and the project's overall health\n- **Map the project** in one call: every map, topic, key, and DITAVAL file, how they connect, and what each deliverable ships\n- **Write reports** as standalone HTML pages, such as a relationship map or a health report\n- **Refactor** by renaming files, keys, and IDs; converting between direct references and keys; extracting and inlining reuse; and splitting topics\n- **Edit** the structure of maps and relationship tables\n- **Control the editor** by opening documents, moving the cursor, selecting elements, and reading the outline\n- **Review** proposals, add comments, revert what an agent wrote, and read the audit log\n- **Publish** deliverables and start or stop a live preview\n\nWhen a client connects, the server tells the assistant where to start: the project health check for any validation or audit question, the project graph for structure, and the report tool for a page. An assistant that follows it finds the checks the editor already has instead of writing its own.\n\nEvery call is attributed to a session, so the audit log records which assistant did what.\n\n## If the token changes {#if-the-token-changes}\n\nRegenerating the token invalidates any client configured with the old one:\n\n```bash\ndogsbay-xml status --reset-token\n```\n\nRestart the editor, and give clients the new configuration.\n\n## Related {#related}\n\n:::cards\n- **[The agent, and what it may touch](/dogsbay-xml-docs/agent/overview){icon=\"shield\"}**\n  Tiers, the write gate, and the audit log, which also apply here.\n\n- **[The command line](/dogsbay-xml-docs/reference/cli){icon=\"terminal\"}**\n  The same operations, without a client.\n:::\n","mdUrl":"https://dogsbay.ai/dogsbay-xml-docs/automation/mcp.md"} ?? undefined;
